{
 "_about": "The cut system. \"directions\" = the 8 cut directions of the owner's clock sheet (Sens de la coupe). \"kata\" = the 12 ZNKR seitei iaido kata as sequences of those cuts. Every pose name is in library/poses.json; \"move\" names are in library/moves.json. Guide: docs/ANIMATION.md §11.",
 "frame": "Target lines are in the OPPONENT's local frame, metres: origin on the ground under the hips, +x = the opponent's own left, +y up, +z = the way the opponent faces. To get world space for an opponent at (X, Z) facing ry: world = [X + x·cos(ry) + z·sin(ry), y, Z − x·sin(ry) + z·cos(ry)]. Lines are for a STANDING paper figure (1.78 m); several kata are aimed at a seated enemy in the original (see each \"enemy\").",
 "attackerFrame": "Pose notes use the attacker's frame: +x = the attacker's left, +z forward. \"From the attacker's upper right\" therefore lands on the opponent's LEFT side.",
 "confidence": {
  "documented": "The eight basic cut directions and the names kiri-oroshi, kesa, yoko, kiri-age, tsuki are standard teaching in kenjutsu and iaido. The 12 seitei kata, their names, order, starting postures, enemy positions and cut sequence come from the All Japan Kendo Federation (ZNKR) iaido manual (7 kata set in 1969, 3 added in 1980, 2 in 2000). They were written here from memory of that manual: check the current edition before quoting a detail (especially left / right of a line and the exact landmark).",
  "tradition": "The clock-face chart with exactly eight numbered cuts is a teaching convention (happo-giri); schools number and name the diagonals differently (see gyaku-kesa nameNote). The reasons given for each kata (who attacks, why) are the manual's scenario, not history.",
  "ours": "Everything in metres or frames: landmark coordinates on our paper body, the tip-path notes, the frame timings (24 fps), the wrist values and the pose names. These are animation choices, not measurements of real practice. Hand/blade poses were solved numerically for our rig (the sword sits at 45° in the hand), then checked on stills."
 },
